1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,880
You understand entities, lifecycle, and
the persistence context. You still do not
want every berth planner use case to
inject EntityManager and write the same
find/save/delete again.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:25,280
Spring Data JPA repositories are the typed
façade — interfaces that become working
beans at startup. Declare what you need
for vessels. Spring Data supplies the
implementation. Look at this java for a
moment.

3
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:33,479
JpaRepository already gives you save,
saveAll, findById, findAll, deleteById,
pagination, and flush helpers.

4
00:00:33,599 --> 00:00:42,999
Derived query methods parse the method
name: findByImoNumber becomes a query on
the imoNumber property — which maps to
column imo_number.

5
00:00:43,119 --> 00:00:52,159
You stay in the entity model — property
names, not column names — until you drop
to @Query or native SQL on purpose. How
does the proxy work?

6
00:00:52,339 --> 00:01:01,259
At startup, Spring Data scans for
repository interfaces, creates fragments
backed primarily by SimpleJpaRepository,
and registers them as beans.

7
00:01:01,379 --> 00:01:07,819
Method calls bind parameters, create a
query, and execute through the
EntityManager tied to the current
transaction.

8
00:01:07,939 --> 00:01:15,859
Exceptions translate into Spring's
DataAccessException hierarchy so harbor
services are not coupled to vendor
SQLState codes.

9
00:01:15,979 --> 00:01:28,819
Pagination belongs in the repository
contract when vessel directories grow:
Look at this java for a moment. Pageable
controls limit, offset, and sort.
Returning Page also runs a count query so
UIs can render total pages.

10
00:01:28,939 --> 00:01:37,859
Returning Slice skips the count when you
only need "has next." Custom
implementations exist when derived names
and @Query are not enough.

11
00:01:37,979 --> 00:01:49,139
Keep the interface clean and add a
fragment class — useful for Criteria or
bulk JDBC — without abandoning the
repository style. Sharp edges show up in
SQL behavior, not bean wiring.

12
00:01:49,259 --> 00:01:58,139
Derived methods that return a single
entity but match multiple rows throw
IncorrectResultSizeDataAccessException —
use List or tighten the predicate.

13
00:01:58,259 --> 00:02:08,859
Modifying queries need @Modifying and a
transactional boundary, and they bypass
dirty checking for entities already in the
persistence context: Look at this java for
a moment.

14
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:22,259
That bulk update returns the number of
rows touched. It is repository work about
vessel rows — not a generic service-wiring
demo. Repositories shine for CRUD and
query intent. They do not replace domain
thinking.

15
00:02:22,379 --> 00:02:35,219
A method named save will happily persist
an invalid Vessel if you never enforced
invariants. Put rules on the entity or in
a domain service; keep the repository as
persistence, not harbor policy.

16
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:47,460
Once your model grows beyond a single
table, repositories alone are not enough.
A manifest is not one row — it is a header
plus cargo lines. Mapping those
associations is the next craft.
